      "writtenOn": "2022-06-24T10:17:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm... This is turning `foo_bar` into `FooBar` and then looking up a `FooBar` function/constructor in the global namespace?\n\nCould we possibly do this in a less black-magic way? A way which makes the dependencies more explicit or at least documents more carefully what is going on :-)\n\nAn alternative could be to have the constructors register themselves somewhere and then pass in the list of known constructors to the `convert_` function here. I typically use a decorator for this:\n\n```\nCONSTRUCTORS \u003d {}\n\ndef constructor(func):\n    camel_case \u003d \u0027\u0027.join(w.title() for w in func.__name__.split(\u0027_\u0027))\n    CONSTRUCTORS[camel_case] \u003d func\n    return func\n\n@constructor\ndef foo_bar(x):\n    print(f\"Constructing a FooBar from {x}\")\n\nif __name__ \u003d\u003d \u0027__main__\u0027:\n    print(CONSTRUCTORS)\n    constructor \u003d CONSTRUCTORS.get(\u0027FooBar\u0027)\n    constructor(42)\n```\n\nThis way there is a link from the constructors to where you look them up: you can find all pieces of code that touch `CONSTRUCTORS` and that should let you quickly establish why a given constructor is or is not present there.",
